xnu: -noprogress also refuses the user-space progress meter

With a graphics boot (boot-args Video.v_display 1) the kernel paints nothing on the
framebuffer once -noprogress keeps its own progress indicator off, but user space can
still switch the meter on: SiliconForest's launchctl sets kern.progressmeterenable=1 and
kern.progressmeter=576 at the start of the system bootstrap, and the kernel then draws
the bar (468x36 pixels at 94% of the height on a 2560x1600 panel) over whatever the
boot loader put there. Seen on the M1 in boots qb01 and qb02: the boot picture painted by
the launcher was untouched except for that bar. With this, -noprogress means no
progress drawing at all; without the boot-arg nothing changes.
UPSTREAM: yes.

--- a/src/Kernel/xnu/osfmk/console/video_console.c
+++ b/src/Kernel/xnu/osfmk/console/video_console.c
@@ -3253,6 +3253,10 @@
 	void    * new_buffer;
 	boolean_t stashBackbuffer;
 	int flags = vinfo.v_rotate;
+	uint32_t notused;
+	// PD: -noprogress also refuses the meter user space asks for (launchctl's boot bar),
+	// so a boot picture put up before the kernel stays as it is
+	boolean_t no_user_meter = PE_parse_boot_argn("-noprogress", &notused, sizeof(notused));
 
 	stashBackbuffer = FALSE;
 	new_buffer = NULL;
@@ -3264,7 +3268,7 @@
 	simple_lock(&vc_progress_lock, LCK_GRP_NULL);
 
 	if (kProgressMeterUser == new_value) {
-		if (gc_enabled || !gc_acquired || !gc_graphics_boot) {
+		if (gc_enabled || !gc_acquired || !gc_graphics_boot || no_user_meter) {
 			new_value = vc_progressmeter_enable;
 		}
 	}
